d it  not always as i want it to  be so uh certainly explore the idea  and uh what states need to do  to reinvigorate the 10th amendment um  as we say in my neck of the woods god  bless texas and  god bless the rest of them as well  sounds like you've already been  challenged and i i think you you  you may have heard a similar question of  the cato institute last fall  in 1971 the us abandoned its obligation  under the bretton woods agreement to  provide redeemability to foreign  governments in gold of u.s currency they  accumulated  since then we've been able to inflate  our currency infinitely because it's  backed by  the petro dollar which we enforce  militarily  the founders very much andrew jackson  when he ended the second bank of the u.s  said that the circulating money of gold  and silver coin  was intended to prevent the wealth of  the laboring class from being inflated  away  by the corporations working with the  politicians and financiers  serving corporatism which it's the  corporatism that's creating the  regulations that empower the  central planning so doesn't it all go  back to following the money like we  learned in watergate and  do we need to restore a constitutional  system of money and also a new bretton  woods agreement  to be fair with other nations my how the  time has flown  a serious subject deep subject one i'm  not  in a position to  address um with the seriousness that it  deserves  at the moment listen we have a number of  challenges  with a fiat currency which our nation  has had for quite some time  and our financial services committee  will do is continuing a lot of work  as part of our federal reserve  centennial oversight  project um  can't completely tell you where  the project is headed and will  ultimately  end we have done our first piece of  legislation one of many to follow  so dealing with sound  money is a very important issue not  prepared to go into it in detail today  i must admit there's a thousand ways to  advance the 